 ## Background
-**Origin:**
-The Euler Method is a first-order numerical integration scheme for univariate functions [S1, unofficial]. It is based on the linearization of the Taylor series expansion [S1, unofficial].
-
-**History and Development:**
-It is the simplest numerical integrator for stream lines, using the current position and vector field value to compute the next step [S2, unofficial]. It is commonly used as a starting point for more advanced integration schemes [S2, unofficial].
-
-**Usage:**
-It is used in vector field visualization, particularly in real-time applications where simplicity is prioritized over accuracy [S2, unofficial]. It serves as a baseline for comparing higher-order methods like Runge-Kutta 4 [S2, unofficial].
+The Euler Method is a first-order numerical integration scheme for univariate functions $x: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^n$, representing a linearization of the Taylor series expansion [S1, unofficial]. It is one of the oldest and simplest methods for solving initial value problems (IVPs) [S1, unofficial]. In the context of vector field visualization, it was implemented early in projects due to its simplicity and the speed of GPUs, which allowed for small time steps to mitigate error [S2, unofficial]. However, it was later found that even with small time steps, Euler integration suffered from qualitative inaccuracies compared to higher-order methods like Runge-Kutta 4 [S2, unofficial].
 
